    dcterms:description "The Techno-Optimist Manifesto (Andreessen, a16z, October 16, 2023) names, in its list of what society has been 'subjected to' in 'a mass demoralization campaign... against technology and against life', both 'Precautionary Principle' and 'existential risk'. Verbatim list as quoted by Hart-Landsberg in Monthly Review 78:3 (2026). Drafted by Claude; awaiting MJSullivan's review before promotion to Curated."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://a16z.com/the-techno-optimist-manifesto/> ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-10-19"^^xsd:date .

tgs:Evidence.GameTheoryFillsTheOughtGap_GameTheory_TGS_126 a thinkr:Evidence, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:confidence thinkr:ConfidenceType.Candidate ;
    thinkr:evidencePolarity thinkr:PolarityType.Qualifies ;
    thinkr:citesEpisode tgs:Interview.TGS_126_AVisionForBetterment ;
    dcterms:description "In TGS_126 Daniel Schmachtenberger treats game theory as an accurate description of how modern institutions choose, because they adopted von Neumann and Morgenstern's formal theory as their working answer to 'what is a good choice?' -- and as a pathology for that same reason. He accepts it as description and rejects it as an ethic, so the contrast with tgs:Concept.GameTheory is partial. Drafted by Claude from the graph's episode records; awaiting MJSullivan's review."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-10-20"^^xsd:date .

tgs:Evidence.GameTheoreticVersusRealValue_GameTheory_TGS_42 a thinkr:Evidence, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:confidence thinkr:ConfidenceType.Candidate ;
    thinkr:evidencePolarity thinkr:PolarityType.Qualifies ;
    thinkr:citesEpisode tgs:Interview.TGS_42_ModelingDriversMetacrisis ;
    dcterms:description "In TGS_42 Daniel Schmachtenberger says 'game theoretical value and real value have a major delta': the payoffs that win competition leave out what we actually value, such as breathable air. Game theory still describes the competition correctly; what he contests is treating its payoffs as value. The contrast with tgs:Concept.GameTheory is partial. Drafted by Claude from the graph's episode records; awaiting MJSullivan's review."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-10-20"^^xsd:date .

tgs:Evidence.Wetware_GameTheory_Frankly_78 a thinkr:Evidence, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:confidence thinkr:ConfidenceType.Candidate ;
    thinkr:evidencePolarity thinkr:PolarityType.Qualifies ;
    thinkr:citesEpisode tgs:Monologue.Frankly_78_ABriefClarificationOnHumanBehavior ;
    dcterms:description "Nate Hagens's account of human wetware -- brains and emotions evolved for small Pleistocene bands -- cuts against classical game theory's rational, calculating player (Frankly_78; in Frankly_76 he adds that in elections emotional, subconscious associations beat higher-level thought). It does not reject strategic analysis; it replaces the assumed player with an evolved, emotional one, the move behavioral game theory makes (tgs:GameTheoryTerm.BehavioralGameTheory). The contrast with tgs:Concept.GameTheory is partial. Drafted by Claude from the graph's episode records; awaiting MJSullivan's review."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-10-20"^^xsd:date .

tgs:Evidence.ProsocialityUnderTheSuperorganism_GameTheory_Frankly_78 a thinkr:Evidence, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:confidence thinkr:ConfidenceType.Candidate ;
    thinkr:evidencePolarity thinkr:PolarityType.Qualifies ;
    thinkr:citesEpisode tgs:Monologue.Frankly_78_ABriefClarificationOnHumanBehavior ;
    dcterms:description "In Frankly_78 Nate Hagens calls Homo economicus, egoistic and narrow, 'a desolate and dismal view' that is untrue: human nature is largely prosocial, held together by strong reciprocity, and the superorganism suppresses it. This contests the self-interest assumption of classical game theory while keeping its structural insight that institutions shape payoffs. The contrast with tgs:Concept.GameTheory is partial. Drafted by Claude from the graph's episode records; awaiting MJSullivan's review."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-10-20"^^xsd:date .

tgs:Evidence.ThirdAttractor_StagHunt_TGS_42 a thinkr:Evidence, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:confidence thinkr:ConfidenceType.Candidate ;
    thinkr:evidencePolarity thinkr:PolarityType.Supports ;
    thinkr:citesEpisode tgs:Interview.TGS_42_ModelingDriversMetacrisis ;
    dcterms:description "Editorial reading, not the speaker's. In TGS_42 Daniel Schmachtenberger frames the third attractor as a coordination system that is 'not corruptible or capturable,' which binds multipolar traps: prisoner's-dilemma logic, solved by governance. Read as a stag hunt, the third attractor is instead a second stable outcome that holds once enough actors trust others to move toward it. The reading fits Nate Hagens's Frankly_78 account of cooperation at the bend-versus-break moment (tgs:Concept.ThreeTimelinesOfResponse) better than Daniel's own framing. Drafted by Claude at MJSullivan's request; awaiting his review."@en ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-10-20"^^xsd:date .

